# Arcs and Readings
#
# How an arc is written as a stream of beats on the canon flow schedule, how a reader keeps personal readings outside the cut, and why the cursor and the charge stay off the arc.

#>spw_arcs_readings
#:layer #!pragmatics
#:domain #!spw
#:form #!protocol
#:level #!advanced
#:review #!spot_checked
#:valence #!boon

^"emit"{
 ~#title: "Arcs and Readings"
 ~#claim: "An arc is an ordered stream of beats a reader walks, and a reading is a note the reader keeps outside the cut that points back in."
 ~#proof: "Canon gives the flow schedule in flow-protocol-sigils; the cut contract keeps readings in reader roots with one-way links."
 ~#door: "Walk one beat: a question about the water density claim, then the claim itself."
 ~#audience: "readers, teachers, and hosts who walk a surface with others"
 continuity: "Arcs and Readings | one-way"
 cold_open: "Walk the beats in order, keep your readings at home, and let every link run one-way into the cut."
}

^"provenance"{
 as_of: "2026-10-02"
 generator: "claude-opus-5.5"
